title Caravan ladder -- the harness answers for a rung that says nothing, and the carry falls to 47
prompt autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first
think Standfast first. Read crux/REDS.md whole: rows %96, %97, %81, %83, %84, %98 all stand CLOSED, and the living ledger reads verdict=ok at 5 rows. No open agent-closable red, so the line was clear for constructive work.
think The running thread came from the newest three logs. The ladder lap at 20260820.181727 lifted eight chained bodies and closed on a fork: close the correspondence arc with a new rung above answer, or reshape the stub itself, which it named as the only move left that lowers the carry.
think Lindy-first, crux-first chose between them. A new rung is one more feature; the stub reshape changes what every future rung costs, so it carries the longer return. It is also the crux in the strict sense -- the hardest still-solvable move, and the one that opens the rest, since the ceiling refuses rather than advises.
think Read the stub before designing against it. A stub exists for exactly one reason: a lifted body re-enters the rung by name on every chained link, and a name a rung never publishes is a compile error. So the rung published a name for every check it merely climbed to. Silence had no way of being heard.
think The design followed from that sentence. Give silence a meaning: reach each link through a helper that runs the rung's own body when @hasDecl finds one, and the harness's own body when it does not. The test is comptime, so the untaken branch is never analyzed and a name the harness lacks can only be reached for by a rung that declares it -- which is exactly how a chain's terminal links keep their own bodies.
think Wrote the brief before the cut, named both limits in it, then seated link and link_wire and converted all 58 re-entry sites.
obs The compiler taught the one thing the brief had not foreseen, and it taught it in a single pass. A harness stub is reached from two directions -- by the harness on a chained link, and by the rung above, whose fold-A delegation names the check on the rung below. Thirteen builds went RED until eleven of those delegations moved to the harness form, `return ladder_checks.check_swell(owing_rung);`, which runs the same body against the same rung and still steps down exactly one rung.
obs That form change reached the one-step guard, and this is the part worth naming twice. The reach scan read only the elder form, so a rung folding entirely in the harness form would have shown no fold at all and passed unguarded -- a count that cannot see what it measures, the REDS %97 shape, arriving by a door nobody had opened yet. Taught the scan both forms and proved it on two new three-rung corpora in the harness shape: one walking down a rung at a time and welcomed, one reaching past its neighbor and refused by name.
obs A first cut was backed out whole rather than repaired. Its regex rewrote definition headers into nonsense like `pub fn ladder_checks.check_unlost(@This(), sys: ...)`, so the working tree was reset to HEAD, the harness change alone restored, and the whole pass redone with the match anchored to `@This()` -- the one token that tells a forwarding stub from a fold-A delegation. Reading the diff before believing the count is what caught it.
obs The measurement moves, and the two numbers that stand still are what make it a finding. The carry falls 2,762 to 47 -- one copied body across 97 modules and 1,091 checks -- as 612 stubs leave the ladder. Fold A holds at exactly 757, counted in both forms, which is the honest signal that a delegation moving between forms is a move rather than a loss. Fold B stands at 69 bodies in the harness, unchanged, since nothing lifted this lap.
obs Fold C is a wall rather than a ratchet, and it is stated that way: zero rungs carry a stub that only forwards itself to the harness, and it stays zero. The ceiling of 4,000 stops being the thing that refuses first, so a rung now costs the ladder its own new checks and nothing else -- which is what folds A and B were always reaching for.
obs Nothing observable changed, and the shape of the change is why. A rung that keeps a body is dispatched to that body exactly as before; a rung that publishes nothing reaches the same harness body one call earlier, and the comptime test makes the two the same machine code. Proven rather than argued: 33 touched rungs built and run from a cold tree, then the whole choir at 91 GREEN.
obs Docs moved in the same commit rather than a later sweep. The README's harness paragraph, its meter section, the copy scan's header and its ceiling note, and the reach scan's header each named a standing or a single fold form that stopped being true the moment the stubs left.
obs The rota doc read this lap was growing a language, and it named the method the lap used without being asked: diverge by strengthening rather than rewriting, each change additive and behavior-preserving, each proven against the foundation by a gate. Fold C changed what the code says and not one thing about what it does, and the gate said so on metal.
loom witness=caravan_suite_witness rungs=91 wall_s=248 verdict=GREEN cold_tree=yes
loom meter=caravan_ladder_copy carried_lines=47 prior=2762 modules=97 checks=1091 fold_a=757 fold_b=69 fold_c_stubs=0
file active-designing/20260820-182533_caravan-ladder-the-harness-answers-for-silence.md the fold C design call, its two named limits, and what actually ran
file caravan/ladder_checks.rye link and link_wire seated; all 58 chained re-entry sites reach through them
file caravan/answer.rye 58 forwarding stubs removed; the self-test reaches the harness directly
file tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_reach_scan.sh reads both fold forms, so a move between them can never blind the one-step guard
file tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_reach_harness_corpus/upper.rye the harness form welcomed, proven on three rungs
file tools/fixtures/caravan_ladder_reach_harness_skip_corpus/upper.rye the harness form refused when it skips a neighbor
file tools/caravan_ladder_copy_witness.rish seven claims now, fold C among them as a wall at zero
file caravan/README.md the harness paragraph and the meter section name the new standing
recommend keep-going the ladder's cost model is answered, and a rung now costs only what it invents. The next constructive door is the arc's own, where the last two laps pointed: a correspondence that reached a person and heard them answer can be closed -- the reader's word either lifting the wall or letting it stand. The meter no longer refuses first; 47 lines stand against a ceiling of 4,000.
